From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hunter B Hough <git@hough.ink>
Date: Tue, 6 Oct 2026 14:36:14 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 117/148] fix(refinery): guard patrol rm targets with ${NAME:?}
 so fences run verbatim (gcp-s7j4.6)

Claude Code's built-in safety check denies an rm whose target is an
unguarded shell variable, because an unset or empty variable would turn it
into a removal at the filesystem root. A refinery session has no person to
approve the prompt, so the command waits out a 2 minute timeout and does not
run, and sessions then hand-edit or retype the fence before running it.
During the first acceptance_check=warn window this meant most sessions
stopped running the acceptance-check fence as written, and several hardcoded
the script path, which skips the formula's locator and its not-runnable WARN.

Each of the six rm commands in mol-refinery-patrol now takes a "${NAME:?}"
target, the form the check exempts by its own message. Every line stays an
rm -f, with no trash and no find -delete, so the form stays portable. Where
the variable is set, which holds at all six lines, behavior is unchanged.
Two lines change shape beyond the guard:
- the prose instruction now sets BATCH_DIR itself, so its target is a
  variable rather than a command substitution;
- the duplicate check wraps its rm in an if [ -n "$DUP_ERR" ], so an empty
  DUP_ERR (mktemp failed) skips the rm instead of aborting the block halfway,
  which a careless retry turns into a duplicate bead.

case_rm_targets_guarded fails, naming each line, if any rm target starts with
"$ and then a letter, underscore or (. It was written first and fails at
f10c843f listing 324, 366, 389, 515, 656 and 1114, and it also proves it can
fail by unguarding one target in a copy of the formula. The byte-exact fence
pin in write_fence takes the new acceptance-check line.

Validation: test_refinery_acceptance_wiring.sh, test_refinery_batch_wiring.sh
and test_refinery_merge_batch.sh each exit 0.

Out of scope: the witness and deacon patrols have the same unguarded shape
but show no denials, so they are unchanged.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
